; The console, standing on end the way the stand holds it: 44 x 215.4 x 157 mm is
; the real machine's own footprint, read as W x H x D in THIS orientation. Origin at
; the base of the stand, so the unit rests on a shelf at y 0 — the same convention
; pc_tower.tscn uses, and not the centred box every placeholder console wears.
;
; The body spans y 0.020 .. 0.2354. Every position on the front face below was scaled
; off a photograph of the real panel, quoted in the comments as millimetres UP FROM
; THE FOOT of that face so the two can be compared directly: model y = 0.020 + mm/1000.
;
; The shell is NOT a box — its front-left vertical edge is chamfered 7 mm the whole
; height of the case, which is why it is baked by Tools/gen_wii_body.gd rather than
; being a BoxMesh here. That cut takes the flat front face from 44 mm down to 37 mm,
; running x -0.015 .. +0.022, and everything printed on it below had to move right to
; stay on it.


; The disc slot, and it is LONG — 176 mm of surround down the right-hand side of the
; face, running from 20 mm up to 196 mm. Far longer than the 120 mm disc that goes
; through it, which is styling rather than mechanism, and the single feature that
; most makes the front read as this machine.
[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="Mesh_slot_recess"]
size = Vector3(0.012, 0.128, 0.0015)

[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="Mesh_slot_glow"]
size = Vector3(0.006, 0.125, 0.001)

[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="Mesh_slot"]
size = Vector3(0.005, 0.125, 0.002)

; The three keys and the seam plate behind each are BAKED by Tools/gen_wii_body.gd,
; not boxes: each one runs ACROSS the corner and bends through the chamfer, so part
; of every key lies on the 45 degree facet, the way the machine's do. Their meshes
; carry the fold at their own origin, which is why each is placed here at the fold
; line itself — x -0.015 — rather than at its centre.
;
; The seams exist because a key drawn in the case's own white vanishes into it —
; these rooms light with a flat ambient and cast no shadow, so there is nothing to
; draw the join. The real panel shows its keys by a fine dark seam all round; each
; plate is the same shape a fraction larger, and that border IS the gap.
; The standby light, which lives ON the power key at its left end — not the whole key
; the way this model first drew it.
[sub_resource type="CylinderMesh" id="Mesh_led"]
top_radius = 0.0011
bottom_radius = 0.0011
height = 0.0008

; The SD door and what it hides. 22 x 90 mm, which is most of the middle of the face:
; it is a big flap on the real machine too. It really opens — see
; RetroSystemModelWii._build_sd_door — so the card slot and the SYNC button behind it
; only exist once it is swung.
;
; The card slot is VERTICAL: a card goes in on its edge, because the machine it goes
; into is standing on its edge.
[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="Mesh_sd_slot"]
size = Vector3(0.004, 0.028, 0.0012)

[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="Mesh_cap_sync"]
size = Vector3(0.015, 0.0075, 0.0022)

