; Where a disc enters, and the pose the seated disc inherits. Rz(-90), so the slot's
; local -Z still points into the machine (the axis MediaSlot rides the disc along)
; while its local +Y lands on the model's +X — which stands the disc on edge, in the
; plane of the machine's own sides, the only way a 120 mm disc fits a 44 mm case.
; Turned a further 180 about its own local Z, which is the INSERTION axis — MediaSlot
; rides the disc along local -Z and Rz leaves that alone, so the disc still goes in the
; same way and only turns over. Rz(180) negates local Y, and local Y is the disc's face
; normal, so this is what puts the printed side up: before it, a disc slid in label-down.
;
; Written as matrix ROWS, so the nine numbers read as the transpose of the basis
; vectors x (0,-1,0), y (1,0,0), z (0,0,1).
[node name="DiscSeat" type="Marker3D" parent="Front"]
transform = Transform3D(0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0.0148, 0.08018, 0.10770)

; --- the GameCube bay, on the +Y face ------------------------------------------
; Laid down by the root's Rz(+90) this face becomes the machine's LEFT side. It is
; 44 x 215.4, which is what finally lets the flaps sit at TRUE SIZE — read from the
; front (+Z) edge:
;   14.0  mm  plain case
;   123.0 mm  controller flap
;   64.34 mm  memory card flap
;   12.5  mm  plain case
; 213.84 against the face's 215.4, so the trailing band carries the 1.56 mm of slack.
;
;   controller flap  z  0.0937 .. -0.0293   centre  0.0322
;   memory flap      z -0.0293 .. -0.0936   centre -0.0615
;
; Both 36 mm across the 44 mm face, which is a guess and the one number here that is.
[node name="Top" type="Node3D" parent="."]

